    "available_at": "2026-11-24T16:36:53.199865+00:00",
    "body": "View of the Ruby Hill property in Nevada, which includes the Archimedes underground mine. Credit: i-80 Gold.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 24, 2026\nI-80 Gold (TSX: IAU; NYSE: IAUX) has begun mining at Archimedes, its second underground gold mine in Nevada, saying it expects to recover the first Archimedes gold from its Ruby Hill leach pad in next year’s first quarter.\nMost of the material mined next year should be oxide that i-80 can leach on site near Eureka, the company said Monday . Its earlier plan called for mostly sulphide material processed by a third party, with payment for 58% of the contained gold. I-80 now plans to stockpile refractory sulphide material for its own Lone Tree plant, which is scheduled for commissioning in the fourth quarter of 2027.\n“A lower-cost oxide route supports near-term cash flow ahead of Lone Tree, though the contribution remains subject to quantification with 2027 guidance,” National Bank Financial mining analyst Don DeMarco wrote in a note Tuesday.\nSome of the oxide lies outside the mineral resource estimate supporting the preliminary economic assessment and hasn’t been classified as resources or reserves. I-80 expects to issue production guidance early next year. Expanded surface infrastructure is expected to lift total project capital to approximately $57.3 million (C$82 million), about 21% more than the $47.3-million estimate that was contained in a March 2025 assessment.\nRuby Hill property\nArchimedes is part of the wholly owned Ruby Hill property, which i-80 acquired in 2021. Construction of the underground project began in September 2025. The company has completed about 3,600 metres of lateral development, and work on additional ventilation, change facilities and offices will continue through 2027 as mining rates increase.\nThe underground mine targets the 426 and Ruby Deeps zones. The 2025 assessment outlined roughly a decade of mining and average annual gold output of about 100,000 oz. after ramp-up. The company reported indicated resources of 1.8 million tonnes grading 7.6 grams gold per tonne and inferred resources of 4.2 million tonnes grading 7.3 grams gold per tonne as of the end of 2025.\nHeap leaching provides a route to recover gold from oxide material before Lone Tree is ready. Refractory sulphide material needs more intensive treatment. I-80 expects a significant oxide contribution at Archimedes over the next three years, although the size of that contribution has not yet been quantified in production guidance.\nSeven underground rigs are drilling the lower 426 and Ruby Deeps zones in a programme of about 55,000 metres across 140 holes. Completion is expected in the second quarter of 2027, supporting a feasibility study in the second half. After Lone Tree is commissioned, i-80 targets combined annual production of 150,000–200,000 oz. from Archimedes and Granite Creek beginning in 2028.\nI-80 shares dropped 1.3% to C$1.52 Tuesday morning in Toronto, valuing the company at about C$1.3 billion ($922 million). The stock has traded between C$1.34 and C$3.04 in the past year.\nPrint\nTAG: gold gold mines gold mining gold production gold projects Nevada\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: United States\nCOMPANY: i-80 Gold\nRepublish this article",
